1
00:00:00,127 --> 00:00:01,975
Picture a World War Two sailor.

2
00:00:02,612 --> 00:00:05,920
You probably see him firing
a huge gun at enemy planes.

3
00:00:06,335 --> 00:00:09,680
But on most days, his guns
never fired a single shot.

4
00:00:10,425 --> 00:00:14,962
So what did three hundred men on one
small steel ship actually do all day?

5
00:00:15,812 --> 00:00:18,018
Let's spend one day aboard and find out.

6
00:00:18,643 --> 00:00:22,586
By the end, you'll see why the most
boring part of that day saved their lives.

7
00:00:23,382 --> 00:00:25,027
Your day doesn't start in the morning.

8
00:00:25,062 --> 00:00:26,691
It starts in the middle of the night.

9
00:00:27,265 --> 00:00:31,245
You're a young sailor on an American
destroyer in the Pacific, in nineteen

10
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:32,317
forty-four.

11
00:00:32,337 --> 00:00:37,010
Sailors called these ships tin cans,
because their steel skin was thin and they

12
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:38,428
rolled with every wave.

13
00:00:39,071 --> 00:00:40,300
Somebody shakes your bunk.

14
00:00:40,784 --> 00:00:44,108
It's a quarter to four in the morning,
and your watch begins at four.

15
00:00:44,813 --> 00:00:47,783
The navy split the day
into four-hour watches.

16
00:00:48,019 --> 00:00:50,579
You stand one watch, then
you get eight hours off.

17
00:00:51,022 --> 00:00:53,012
But those eight hours are full of work.

18
00:00:53,234 --> 00:00:56,496
So a whole night of sleep is
something you simply stop having.

19
00:00:57,221 --> 00:00:58,371
Your bed doesn't help.

20
00:00:58,817 --> 00:01:04,102
It's a canvas rack, stacked three or four
high, in a hot compartment that smells of

21
00:01:04,122 --> 00:01:05,272
oil and sweat.

22
00:01:05,657 --> 00:01:10,072
So in the tropics, many men dragged their
mattresses up and slept on the open deck.

23
00:01:10,691 --> 00:01:12,188
Tonight your job is lookout.

24
00:01:12,856 --> 00:01:17,183
You stand on deck with binoculars and
stare at black water for four hours.

25
00:01:17,783 --> 00:01:21,796
You're searching for a periscope,
a wake, a shadow in the sky.

26
00:01:22,543 --> 00:01:24,292
Most nights, you see nothing at all.

27
00:01:24,855 --> 00:01:29,683
But you can't relax, because the one night
you miss something could be the last night

28
00:01:29,703 --> 00:01:30,853
for everyone.

29
00:01:31,163 --> 00:01:33,442
At dawn, an alarm rings
through the whole ship.

30
00:01:34,024 --> 00:01:35,174
General quarters.

31
00:01:35,710 --> 00:01:37,483
Every man runs to his battle station.

32
00:01:38,036 --> 00:01:39,186
Why every single morning?

33
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:43,601
Because first light was a favorite time
for submarines and planes to attack.

34
00:01:44,202 --> 00:01:46,761
So the whole crew waits at the
guns while the sun comes up.

35
00:01:47,441 --> 00:01:48,883
Usually, nothing happens.

36
00:01:49,353 --> 00:01:54,733
The sky stays empty. The alarm stops, and
three hundred tired men walk to breakfast.

37
00:01:55,298 --> 00:01:56,500
Tomorrow they'll do it again.

38
00:01:57,115 --> 00:01:58,265
Tonight at dusk, too.

39
00:01:58,877 --> 00:02:00,647
Breakfast is on a crowded mess deck.

40
00:02:00,996 --> 00:02:03,900
You eat fast, standing in
line with a steel tray.

41
00:02:04,396 --> 00:02:08,482
Coffee is everywhere, strong
and black, day and night.

42
00:02:08,961 --> 00:02:10,539
But there's no beer to go with it.

43
00:02:10,814 --> 00:02:15,133
The United States Navy had banned alcohol
on its ships back in nineteen fourteen.

44
00:02:15,312 --> 00:02:17,658
So coffee became the
fuel that ran the crew.

45
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:21,424
Now comes the part nobody
puts in war movies.

46
00:02:21,704 --> 00:02:22,717
Work.

47
00:02:22,737 --> 00:02:24,202
The sea is a slow enemy.

48
00:02:24,563 --> 00:02:27,423
Salt water attacks every inch
of steel, every single day.

49
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:32,175
So sailors spend hours chipping old
paint and rust with little hammers.

50
00:02:32,241 --> 00:02:35,455
They paint the spot grey again,
and the salt comes right back.

51
00:02:35,991 --> 00:02:37,141
It never ends.

52
00:02:37,359 --> 00:02:38,509
But there was a twist.

53
00:02:38,872 --> 00:02:42,926
Early in the war, the navy learned that
thick layers of paint burned like fuel

54
00:02:42,946 --> 00:02:44,096
when a ship was hit.

55
00:02:44,244 --> 00:02:47,828
So crews were ordered to strip
paint from inside their own ships.

56
00:02:48,154 --> 00:02:52,356
Imagine scraping paint off your ship in
the middle of a war, because the paint

57
00:02:52,376 --> 00:02:53,560
itself could kill you.

58
00:02:54,487 --> 00:02:56,130
On top of that, there's cleaning.

59
00:02:56,395 --> 00:02:58,970
The navy called a big
cleaning day a field day.

60
00:02:59,317 --> 00:03:04,130
Decks are scrubbed, brass is polished, and
an officer checks every corner for dust.

61
00:03:04,806 --> 00:03:06,880
But a ship in the middle
of the ocean has a problem.

62
00:03:07,146 --> 00:03:10,503
It needs fresh water and fuel,
and there's no shop out there.

63
00:03:10,912 --> 00:03:14,549
Fresh water came from machines
that boiled sea water.

64
00:03:14,593 --> 00:03:18,679
Those machines barely kept up
with the boilers and the galley.

65
00:03:18,699 --> 00:03:20,155
So water for the men came last.

66
00:03:20,319 --> 00:03:23,623
Showers were short, rationed, or done in
salt water that left your skin sticky.

67
00:03:24,446 --> 00:03:26,166
Fuel was an even bigger problem.

68
00:03:26,404 --> 00:03:29,057
A destroyer could burn through
its tanks in a few days.

69
00:03:29,147 --> 00:03:33,521
So it had to pull alongside a much bigger
ship in open sea and take fuel through

70
00:03:33,541 --> 00:03:34,286
hoses.

71
00:03:34,306 --> 00:03:38,083
Two ships rolling side by side,
only a few dozen meters apart.

72
00:03:38,286 --> 00:03:40,711
One wrong turn of the
wheel, and they collide.

73
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:43,868
Running low on fuel was
dangerous in another way.

74
00:03:44,171 --> 00:03:48,302
In December nineteen forty-four, a typhoon
hit the American fleet in the Philippine

75
00:03:48,322 --> 00:03:48,894
Sea.

76
00:03:48,914 --> 00:03:52,660
Some destroyers were low on fuel,
riding high and light in the water.

77
00:03:53,036 --> 00:03:54,790
Three of them rolled over and sank.

78
00:03:55,185 --> 00:03:59,293
About seven hundred and ninety men were
lost in that storm, and the enemy never

79
00:03:59,313 --> 00:04:00,463
fired a shot.

80
00:04:00,875 --> 00:04:02,669
After lunch, the alarm rings again.

81
00:04:03,081 --> 00:04:04,231
But it's not an attack.

82
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:05,570
It's a drill.

83
00:04:05,651 --> 00:04:06,692
Fire drill.

84
00:04:06,712 --> 00:04:07,862
Man overboard drill.

85
00:04:08,130 --> 00:04:09,136
Gun drill.

86
00:04:09,156 --> 00:04:13,761
Damage control drill, where you practice
patching holes and fighting fires in dark,

87
00:04:13,781 --> 00:04:14,864
flooded rooms.

88
00:04:14,884 --> 00:04:17,339
Sailors complained
about drills constantly.

89
00:04:17,373 --> 00:04:20,692
They were hot, exhausting,
and the same every day.

90
00:04:21,025 --> 00:04:24,001
So why practice the same
thing a hundred times?

91
00:04:24,313 --> 00:04:28,028
Hold on to that question, because the
answer comes at the end of this video.

92
00:04:28,894 --> 00:04:31,177
In the evening, the ship
slows down a little.

93
00:04:31,653 --> 00:04:33,041
This is the best part of the day.

94
00:04:33,455 --> 00:04:35,093
Maybe the mail has arrived.

95
00:04:35,729 --> 00:04:39,592
Letters could take weeks to reach a ship,
so when the mail bags came aboard, the

96
00:04:39,612 --> 00:04:40,763
whole crew went quiet.

97
00:04:41,167 --> 00:04:43,151
Many letters arrived as V mail.

98
00:04:43,379 --> 00:04:47,769
These were tiny photographed copies of
letters, shrunk down to save space on

99
00:04:47,789 --> 00:04:48,664
planes.

100
00:04:48,684 --> 00:04:52,304
Men read the same letter again and
again, until the paper was soft.

101
00:04:53,065 --> 00:04:53,913
No mail?

102
00:04:53,933 --> 00:04:58,179
Then there are cards, dice, and a
navy board game called acey-deucey.

103
00:04:58,631 --> 00:05:01,532
Sometimes a movie plays on a
sheet hung across the deck.

104
00:05:01,661 --> 00:05:06,065
The same few films played so often that
sailors could say every line along with

105
00:05:06,085 --> 00:05:07,235
the actors.

106
00:05:07,314 --> 00:05:08,870
There were stranger traditions, too.

107
00:05:09,408 --> 00:05:13,240
When a ship crossed the equator, sailors
who had never crossed it were called

108
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:14,025
pollywogs.

109
00:05:14,045 --> 00:05:18,506
The veterans, called shellbacks, put them
through a day of silly tests, costumes and

110
00:05:18,526 --> 00:05:19,483
dunking.

111
00:05:19,503 --> 00:05:22,373
In the middle of a war, they still
made time for a party at sea.

112
00:05:22,904 --> 00:05:25,165
At sunset comes one last rule.

113
00:05:25,693 --> 00:05:26,843
Darken ship.

114
00:05:27,173 --> 00:05:28,650
Every light on deck goes out.

115
00:05:29,008 --> 00:05:31,702
No flashlights, and no smoking outside.

116
00:05:32,163 --> 00:05:36,076
Because one glowing cigarette could
be seen by a submarine far away.

117
00:05:36,366 --> 00:05:38,706
So the whole ship
disappears into the night.

118
00:05:39,377 --> 00:05:40,282
And you?

119
00:05:40,302 --> 00:05:41,712
You've got the midnight watch.

120
00:05:42,116 --> 00:05:44,754
So your day starts again before
you've had time to sleep.

121
00:05:45,282 --> 00:05:49,331
Watch, alarm, work, drill, letters, watch.

122
00:05:49,678 --> 00:05:50,753
Weeks of it.

123
00:05:50,773 --> 00:05:51,923
Months of it.

124
00:05:52,443 --> 00:05:54,621
But one day was not normal.

125
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:57,673
April sixteenth, nineteen forty-five.

126
00:05:58,099 --> 00:05:59,412
The waters off Okinawa.

127
00:05:59,931 --> 00:06:03,718
The destroyer Laffey is far out
from the fleet on radar picket duty.

128
00:06:04,027 --> 00:06:08,077
Its job is to spot enemy planes
early and warn the ships behind it.

129
00:06:08,653 --> 00:06:11,096
That morning, the planes
came straight for the Laffey.

130
00:06:11,612 --> 00:06:12,650
Dozens of them.

131
00:06:12,670 --> 00:06:17,467
Many were kamikazes, pilots who planned
to crash their planes into ships.

132
00:06:17,813 --> 00:06:21,486
For about eighty minutes, the
Laffey was attacked again and again.

133
00:06:21,795 --> 00:06:25,040
It was hit by six kamikaze
planes and four bombs.

134
00:06:25,207 --> 00:06:26,452
Fires everywhere.

135
00:06:26,572 --> 00:06:28,763
The rudder jammed. Compartments flooded.

136
00:06:28,956 --> 00:06:31,940
Thirty-two men were killed,
and dozens more were wounded.

137
00:06:32,336 --> 00:06:34,004
A ship like that should have sunk.

138
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:36,043
But the Laffey stayed afloat.

139
00:06:36,422 --> 00:06:37,235
Why?

140
00:06:37,255 --> 00:06:39,849
Because its crew had
done all of this before.

141
00:06:40,097 --> 00:06:41,960
Not for real, but in drills.

142
00:06:42,136 --> 00:06:44,575
Again and again, every afternoon.

143
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:47,302
Every man knew where the
hoses were in the dark.

144
00:06:47,457 --> 00:06:50,745
Every man knew which door to
close and which hole to patch.

145
00:06:50,979 --> 00:06:54,728
The boring drills that sailors
hated kept their ship alive.

146
00:06:55,505 --> 00:06:58,437
So what did World War Two
sailors actually do all day?

147
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:01,417
Mostly, they kept their ship alive.

148
00:07:01,531 --> 00:07:06,629
They scraped, cleaned, watched
and practiced, day after day.

149
00:07:06,825 --> 00:07:10,850
Because a sailor's war was months of
boring days, all built for one terrible

150
00:07:10,870 --> 00:07:11,794
hour.

151
00:07:11,814 --> 00:07:13,193
The Laffey survived the war.

152
00:07:13,437 --> 00:07:15,423
Today, you can still walk on its deck.

153
00:07:15,576 --> 00:07:17,407
It's a museum ship in South Carolina.

154
00:07:18,031 --> 00:07:21,362
Sailors at least had a bed and
a hot meal between the alarms.

155
00:07:21,528 --> 00:07:24,584
Bomber crews in England went from a warm
bed to the coldest, deadliest sky of the

156
00:07:24,604 --> 00:07:26,292
war. That's our next video.

157
00:07:26,772 --> 00:07:29,014
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.

